  "prompt_text": "The figure compares the crystal frameworks of the perovskite oxide SrTiO₃ and the related oxide ReO₃, emphasizing how both are built from corner-sharing oxygen octahedra. In SrTiO₃, blue TiO₆ octahedra form a square-like network, with orange oxygen atoms at the octahedral corners and a large gray Sr ion occupying the cavity between neighboring octahedra. This highlights the usual ABO₃ perovskite arrangement, where the A-site cation sits in the spaces created by the octahedral framework.\n\nIn ReO₃, shown in purple, the same kind of corner-sharing octahedral network is preserved, but the central cavity is empty: there is no A-site ion analogous to Sr. The structure is therefore presented as an “A-site vacant” perovskite. The final view extends this idea into three dimensions, showing a continuous, fully connected lattice of ReO₆ octahedra linked through shared oxygen corners throughout the crystal.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that ReO₃ has a perovskite-like architecture closely related to SrTiO₃, but simplified by the absence of the large A-site cation, leaving a rigid 3D network of connected octahedra.",
